WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:03.730
The source begins near two hundred million years ago with Pangaea

00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:05.430
and a surrounding global ocean.

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:09.890
Rifting separates North America from Africa and adjoining southern landmasses.

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:13.690
By roughly one hundred fifty million years ago, a Central Atlantic

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:17.130
basin exists, while much of Gondwana remains connected.

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:19.650
Rifting begins within continental crust;

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:23.910
later spreading creates oceanic crust between the separating landmasses.

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:28.590
Around one hundred twenty million years ago, the reconstruction shows South

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:33.310
America separating from Africa and parts of Gondwana separating toward the

00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:37.150
early Indian Ocean. By ninety million years ago, the South Atlantic

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:40.350
has widened and India is isolated and moving north.

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:43.810
Do not read an oval world reconstruction as only one hemisphere

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:46.410
or assume every Atlantic sector opened simultaneously.

00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:51.570
The northern Atlantic sector opens later in the source sequence as

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:53.850
Greenland and North America separate from Europe.

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:57.670
India then approaches and collides with Asia, consuming much of the

00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:02.170
intervening Tethys Ocean. Africa and other fragments also converge toward southern

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:06.010
Europe. The modern arrangement combines opening oceans with closing oceans;

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:09.830
it is not simply Pangaea being pulled equally in every direction.

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:14.310
Spreading adds oceanic lithosphere, while subduction removes it.

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:18.530
The source contrasts the generally expanding Atlantic with consumption around much

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:21.850
of the Pacific. Earth does not need to expand to accommodate

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:25.630
this exchange. Local exceptions and boundary details still matter:

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:29.230
a broad basin-level pattern does not mean that no subduction occurs

00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:30.530
anywhere near the Atlantic.

00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:35.730
Future reconstructions extend measured plate motions and assumptions about collisions.

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:40.070
Continuing convergence could narrow or close some seas while spreading enlarges

00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:43.070
others, but boundaries and velocities can change.

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:45.490
Learn the relative opening order in this source:

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:49.490
Central Atlantic, then South Atlantic, then the northern sector.

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:53.970
Use approximate source ages as reconstruction landmarks, and keep a conditional

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:56.950
projection separate from an observed past rock record.
